  "published": "2026-09-24T23:51:02.153Z",
  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "LinqContraband.Scan can now gate CI: --fail-on fails the build on findings at a chosen severity, --baseline fails only on new findings, --rules, --skip-rules and --exclude filter the report, --html writes a shareable report, and a GitHub Action (uses: georgepwall1991/LinqContraband@v5.12.0) adds a job summary and pull-request annotations. The text report lists each rule's findings with the line of code. dotnet format analyzers now applies every fix it can, even when a rule's first finding has none (such as an LC009 query whose entities leave the method or an LC007 loop it cannot rewrite). Fixes from scans of real EF Core applications: LC014 no longer hangs the build on query = query.Where(u => u.Name.ToLower() == name); LC009 stays quiet when a helper such as AutoMapper's ProjectTo reshapes the query into DTOs or scalars; LC007 and LC010 stay quiet on Chunk batches and Skip/Take paging loops; LC015 counts a project's own sort helpers as ordering; LC017 no longer reports loads whose entities are changed through a navigation or an entity method, where its fix made SaveChanges save nothing; LC011 reads keys from Entity<T>(b => ...) lambdas, configuration helpers and referenced projects; LC027 reads relationship configuration from OnModelCreating helpers and builder locals; LC040 skips methods that pick one tracking mode per call and non-entity reads; LC024 accepts conversions around aggregates; LC002 allows ToLookup after ToListAsync and names both calls correctly; LC035 counts a project's own predicate-taking Where as a filter; LC041 reports only a materializer that is the local's whole value.",
